\description{
Raw impact estimates (comparisons of animals with and without a disease)
are conflations of the disease's own impact and the impacts of associated
diseases. \code{deconflate()} removes the part of each estimate that belongs to
other diseases, for one impact table at a time: additive impacts (the
default), or event impacts (\code{event_model = TRUE}). When inputs have
distributions, it also runs draws (\code{n_draws}) and reports intervals.
}
\section{The model}{

The package has one model. The joint distribution of disease combinations
is the maximum-entropy distribution that matches the disease
probabilities and every association row (\code{\link[=fit_joint]{fit_joint()}}); pairs that no
row mentions are unknown and are filled in by the fit (listed in
\verb{$unknown_pairs}). Every impact row is then an equation in the unknown
impacts: an impact for each disease and an interaction for each
combination that appears in the impact table (see \code{\link[=cm_impacts]{cm_impacts()}} for what
each row means).

When the impacts are additive and the inputs are only crude pair
associations (one for every pair) and impact rows about
single diseases, the pairwise 2x2 tables give exactly the same answer
(the simultaneous solution of Rasmussen et al. 2022, eq. 14, generalised
to adjusted estimates), so the package uses them ("pairwise shortcut",
recorded in the result), and fits the joint distribution once to confirm
that the associations can hold together (up to 20 diseases). The draws
then use the shortcut too.

The proportional approximation of Rasmussen et al. (2022, eq. 16) is not
part of the model: it is kept for comparison and reproduction in
\code{\link[=compare_methods]{compare_methods()}} and the \verb{reproduce_*()} functions.
}

\section{Additive impacts}{

With impacts \code{b} and interactions \code{delta}, the outcome of an animal with
disease combination \code{c} is \verb{y(c) = sum_d b_d c_d + sum_T delta_T all_T(c)}.
Each impact row is a linear function of \code{b} and \code{delta}: for a crude row,
the difference in mean outcome between animals with all the row's
diseases and animals with none of them (within \code{among}); for an adjusted
row, a regression coefficient. Results are in the units of the impacts.

The expected aggregate impact per animal is
\verb{sum_d p_d b_d + sum_T P(T) delta_T}. Each disease's contribution is its
own term plus an equal share of each interaction it is part of (the
closed-form Shapley value); contributions add up to the aggregate. Shares
are \code{NA} when the aggregate is zero.
}

\section{Several rows per item}{

Impact rows are fitted together: with one row per unknown the equations
are solved exactly; with more rows, by weighted least squares with weights
1/SD^2 (SDs from each row's distribution; a row without a distribution is
exact and is reproduced exactly). The result reports each row's fitted
value and standardised residual (\verb{$estimates}) and the heterogeneity
statistic Q (\verb{$heterogeneity}). Two exact rows that contradict each other
are an error.

Association rows that describe the same quantity are pooled on the log
odds ratio scale of their 2x2 table (the logit of a prevalence, the log
of an \code{OR_ratio}, or, for rows of different kinds about the same
quantity, the logit of its probability), inverse-variance weighted, with random effects (DerSimonian-Laird) by
default and \code{pool = "fixed"} as an option; Q, I^2 and tau^2 are in
\verb{$heterogeneity}. A row without a distribution is exact and wins. In the
draws, each row is drawn from its own distribution and pooled with the
central weights, and a between-study term keeps the pooled variance at
its random-effects value.
}

\section{Event impacts}{

With \code{event_model = TRUE}, the impact table holds event impacts (hazard
ratios, rate ratios, risk ratios, odds ratios or risk differences; see
\code{\link[=cm_impacts]{cm_impacts()}}), which are adjusted with the snapshot hazard model. Within
the period of the overall risk, an animal with disease combination \code{c} has
a constant hazard \verb{h0 * exp(sum_d beta_d c_d + sum_T gamma_T all_T(c))}, so
its risk over the period is \code{R(c) = 1 - exp(-h0 * exp(...))}; \code{h0} is set
so that the population risk equals \code{overall_risk}. The \code{beta}s and
\code{gamma}s (interactions on the log-hazard scale) are solved so that each
raw estimate is reproduced: a hazard or rate ratio by the ratio of the
average hazard multipliers of the two groups the row compares; a risk
ratio, odds ratio or risk difference by those of their average risks;
crude, or within strata of the row's adjustment set (combined with
Mantel-Haenszel-type weights). The adjusted hazard ratios \code{exp(beta)} are
each disease's own hazard multiplier. The risk attributable to disease is
\code{overall_risk} minus the disease-free risk \code{1 - exp(-h0)}, allocated to
diseases by Shapley values over disease combinations (\code{\link[=shapley_by_cell]{shapley_by_cell()}}).

What the snapshot model is not: a Cox hazard ratio estimated over
follow-up is not, in general, the snapshot ratio, because animals with
high hazards leave first and the mixture of disease combinations among
survivors changes. The model is exact for its own comparisons and a
reasonable approximation when follow-up is short relative to the hazards
or the diseases are rare. Risk-based estimates (risk ratios, odds ratios,
risk differences) must refer to the same period as \code{overall_risk}.
}

\section{Uncertainty}{

Inputs with a distribution (the \code{dist} columns of the input tables, or
\code{distributions} in \code{\link[=cm_model]{cm_model()}}; and \code{overall_risk} given as a
distribution) are drawn \code{n_draws} times; each draw is adjusted in the same
way as the central estimate. The central estimate uses the point values
(\code{value}, and the mean of an \code{overall_risk} distribution); the draws give
95\% intervals (2.5\% and 97.5\% quantiles), means, Monte Carlo standard
errors and stability checks (\verb{$draws$summary}). Draws whose values cannot
hold together are rejected and counted (\verb{$draws$rejections}), not
replaced; notes in \verb{$notes} report a high rejection share and limited
Monte Carlo precision. Without distributions, no draws are run.
}

\section{Errors and warnings}{

Errors and warnings are condition objects with classes (see
\link{deconflate-package}). Those about particular inputs also hold them as
fields, so that code can use them without reading the message:
\itemize{
\item \code{table} and \code{row} (or \code{rows}): the input rows concerned, e.g. an impact
row that is not identifiable, or exact rows that contradict each other
(with their \code{residuals} for impact rows);
\item \code{terms}: diseases and interactions, e.g. those the impact rows do not
determine (\code{deconflate_singular}), or those whose adjusted impact
changes sign (the warning \code{deconflate_sign_change});
\item \code{triples}: the conflicting triples when the crude pair rows cannot all
hold (\code{deconflate_infeasible}), as in \code{\link[=check_feasibility]{check_feasibility()}};
\item \code{pairs}: the pairs without a crude pair row, for the calculations that
need every pair (\code{deconflate_unknown_pairs});
\item \code{residual}: how far a fit was from converging
(\code{deconflate_nonconvergence}).
}
}

